  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 131 PM CDT Fri Sep 18 2026\n\nAs a low pressure system moves out of southeastern SD/northeastern \nNE this afternoon and evening, it will be taking the rain showers \nwith it. As of 1pm, these rain showers have mostly moved out of \nnortheastern SD, with some lingering showers for another hour or so \nin the area. While there have been some stronger winds occasionally \nat the surface with this low pressure, with gusts of 25 to 30 mph, \nthe winds aloft and at the surface have not been strong enough to \npush the low cloud deck out of central and northeastern SD as the \nrain has moved out. This stratus deck has been causing some misty \nconditions through this morning and will continue into the afternoon \nover central and northeastern SD and west central MN. The radar has \nbeen able to pick up on these misty conditions closer to the radar \nas the lower clouds have been able to be detected on the radar beam. \nHowever, even though there are misty conditions over central SD, the \nradar is not able to pick up on them because the clouds are lower \nthan the radar beam. There will be lower visibilities because of the \nlow clouds and mist, though visibilities should stay above 1 mile.\n\nThese misty conditions and low clouds should dissipate and move out \nSaturday morning over central SD. Despite central SD clearing out, \nnortheastern SD could see these conditions worsen as fog develops \novernight into Saturday morning along the eastern edge and over the \nPrairie Coteau. Visibilities in this area could get below a mile at \ntimes. Some cooler air will be sticking around through the weekend \nand into the beginning of next week, with temperatures at the \nsurface being around normal to about 5-10 degrees cooler than \nnormal. Highs will be in the 60s, with lows getting in the mid 40s \nto low 50s after tonight. \n\nAn upper-level low and shortwave over the Pacific northwest will be \nmoving east Saturday and over SD Sunday into Monday. This shortwave \nwill help to push a lee of the Rockies surface trough from WY to \nover western SD Saturday evening into Sunday. With ample moisture \nsticking around the state through the weekend, the lifting from this \nsurface trough will be able to develop some rain showers. These rain \nshowers will stay out of central SD through most of the day Saturday \nas they sit in western SD until Saturday evening when some scattered \nshowers start to move over areas west of the Missouri River. These \nshowers will slowly spread east over the rest of central SD \novernight into Sunday morning and then over portions of northeastern \nSD Sunday afternoon. These rain showers then look to move out of \ncentral and northeastern SD late Sunday night/early Monday morning.",
